WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:04.224
In this video you'll learn what Loom is,

2
00:00:04.224 --> 00:00:08.020
where it came from, and how its interface works.

3
00:00:08.020 --> 00:00:12.374
Loom is a tree-based interface for base models:

4
00:00:12.374 --> 00:00:15.695
at any point it shows several continuations,

5
00:00:15.695 --> 00:00:18.237
and you choose which branch to follow,

6
00:00:18.237 --> 00:00:20.455
edit, or grow.

7
00:00:20.455 --> 00:00:25.436
It was built by janus, who posts as @repligate.

8
00:00:25.436 --> 00:00:29.418
The last video drew the tree of possible texts.

9
00:00:29.418 --> 00:00:32.786
Loom is a way to walk around inside it.

10
00:00:33.236 --> 00:00:37.996
janus learned of GPT-3 in the summer of 2020

11
00:00:37.996 --> 00:00:41.015
and used it through AI Dungeon, which she calls

12
00:00:41.015 --> 00:00:43.894
the only public way in at the time.

13
00:00:43.894 --> 00:00:46.425
She let the model write mostly on its own,

14
00:00:46.425 --> 00:00:49.014
and retried, cut short,

15
00:00:49.014 --> 00:00:52.183
or edited outputs that went wrong.

16
00:00:52.183 --> 00:00:53.507
But in her words,

17
00:00:53.507 --> 00:00:57.338
"Adventures on AI Dungeon are single-history":

18
00:00:57.338 --> 00:01:00.055
each new try replaced the last.

19
00:01:00.055 --> 00:01:02.644
So she saved other branches by hand,

20
00:01:02.644 --> 00:01:04.536
first in linked documents,

21
00:01:04.536 --> 00:01:07.045
then as a tree in a whiteboard app.

22
00:01:07.045 --> 00:01:08.507
Next she built a web app

23
00:01:08.507 --> 00:01:11.839
that ran many AI Dungeon games at once:

24
00:01:11.839 --> 00:01:15.415
up to a dozen continuations, side by side.

25
00:01:15.415 --> 00:01:18.933
Once OpenAI gave her direct access to the model,

26
00:01:18.933 --> 00:01:20.814
she began building Loom.

27
00:01:20.814 --> 00:01:24.448
She wrote it up on her website in February 2021;

28
00:01:24.448 --> 00:01:27.318
its code is open source on GitHub.

29
00:01:27.767 --> 00:01:31.331
Everything in Loom is a node: a piece of text.

30
00:01:31.331 --> 00:01:33.897
A node's parent is the text before it,

31
00:01:33.897 --> 00:01:36.974
and its children are possible next pieces.

32
00:01:36.974 --> 00:01:39.969
Press G, and Loom asks the model for several

33
00:01:39.969 --> 00:01:43.766
continuations of a set length at once,

34
00:01:43.766 --> 00:01:45.530
each a new child.

35
00:01:45.530 --> 00:01:47.667
Read them, pick one,

36
00:01:47.667 --> 00:01:49.965
and generate again from there.

37
00:01:49.965 --> 00:01:52.218
Or go back to any earlier node

38
00:01:52.218 --> 00:01:54.296
and branch there instead.

39
00:01:54.296 --> 00:01:56.072
The other children stay in the tree,

40
00:01:56.072 --> 00:01:57.872
so nothing is lost.

41
00:01:57.872 --> 00:02:01.099
Any node can also be edited by hand:

42
00:02:01.099 --> 00:02:03.885
fix a word, or add half a sentence.

43
00:02:03.885 --> 00:02:05.940
janus calls the core of her method

44
00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:09.075
"manual iterative rejection sampling":

45
00:02:09.075 --> 00:02:13.185
ask for continuations, fewer than five on average,

46
00:02:13.185 --> 00:02:15.310
sometimes over a hundred, keep one,

47
00:02:15.310 --> 00:02:17.957
reject the rest, and go on.

48
00:02:17.957 --> 00:02:21.801
She built Loom to make that loop fast.

49
00:02:22.252 --> 00:02:25.213
Loom shows one tree in several ways.

50
00:02:25.213 --> 00:02:27.082
Read mode shows the current node

51
00:02:27.082 --> 00:02:31.006
and its ancestors as one continuous document,

52
00:02:31.006 --> 00:02:34.175
with an outline of the tree in a sidebar.

53
00:02:34.175 --> 00:02:35.801
Visualize mode draws

54
00:02:35.801 --> 00:02:38.587
the multiverse as a tree diagram;

55
00:02:38.587 --> 00:02:41.211
zoom out to see its overall shape.

56
00:02:41.211 --> 00:02:43.464
The keyboard moves you through it:

57
00:02:43.464 --> 00:02:47.144
right goes to a child, left to the parent,

58
00:02:47.144 --> 00:02:49.837
up and down to the siblings.

59
00:02:49.837 --> 00:02:52.682
Loom also keeps each generated

60
00:02:52.682 --> 00:02:54.957
token's probability:

61
00:02:54.957 --> 00:02:57.372
it can shade the text by how strongly

62
00:02:57.372 --> 00:02:59.624
the model predicted each token,

63
00:02:59.624 --> 00:03:01.563
and you can click a token to swap

64
00:03:01.563 --> 00:03:04.326
in one of the model's alternatives.

65
00:03:04.326 --> 00:03:06.985
Its block multiverse view

66
00:03:06.985 --> 00:03:10.282
lays future text out as blocks,

67
00:03:10.282 --> 00:03:12.256
left to right in time,

68
00:03:12.256 --> 00:03:15.100
each as tall as its probability.

69
00:03:15.100 --> 00:03:17.573
Click one, and the view zooms in,

70
00:03:17.573 --> 00:03:20.977
as if that text had already been chosen.

71
00:03:21.427 --> 00:03:23.621
Later versions of Loom can also

72
00:03:23.621 --> 00:03:25.374
use other model providers,

73
00:03:25.374 --> 00:03:28.567
and models running on your own computer.

74
00:03:28.567 --> 00:03:33.002
By early 2023, the AI safety company Conjecture

75
00:03:33.002 --> 00:03:36.509
was hosting a version of Loom called Bonsai.

76
00:03:36.509 --> 00:03:39.620
That year, a developer called celeste started

77
00:03:39.620 --> 00:03:42.964
a Loom inside the note-taking app Obsidian,

78
00:03:42.964 --> 00:03:45.750
and janus has contributed to it.

79
00:03:45.750 --> 00:03:49.164
Base models, meanwhile, got harder to reach.

80
00:03:49.164 --> 00:03:54.063
OpenAI retired the original GPT-3 base models

81
00:03:54.063 --> 00:03:58.312
in January 2024, and scheduled its last two

82
00:03:58.312 --> 00:04:01.333
to shut down in September 2026.

83
00:04:01.912 --> 00:04:04.489
janus has written that she almost always

84
00:04:04.489 --> 00:04:07.902
uses base models, because their randomness

85
00:04:07.902 --> 00:04:11.873
is what makes steering by choice possible.

86
00:04:11.873 --> 00:04:13.777
Base models with open weights

87
00:04:13.777 --> 00:04:15.484
can still be run locally.

88
00:04:15.484 --> 00:04:19.118
Every pick in Loom is a choice among branches.

89
00:04:19.118 --> 00:04:21.800
The next video measures how much

90
00:04:21.800 --> 00:04:24.936
one choice can steer, in bits.

91
00:04:25.385 --> 00:04:27.475
Keep this. One.

92
00:04:27.475 --> 00:04:30.842
Loom shows the multiverse as a tree you can walk:

93
00:04:30.842 --> 00:04:33.326
generate several children, pick one,

94
00:04:33.326 --> 00:04:37.425
go back and branch, or edit by hand. Two.

95
00:04:37.425 --> 00:04:40.292
Loom grew out of janus's own practice.

96
00:04:40.292 --> 00:04:42.069
She built it to speed up a loop

97
00:04:42.069 --> 00:04:44.240
she already ran by hand:

98
00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:49.640
generate many, keep one, go on. Three.

99
00:04:50.103 --> 00:04:53.447
Loom is meant for base models, whose variety

100
00:04:53.447 --> 00:04:56.525
makes the branches worth choosing between.
